<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <parent>
        <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-clients</artifactId>
        <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
        <version>0.6.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    </parent>
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <name>Parallel Consumer - Proxy Client Kotlin</name>
    <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-client-kotlin</artifactId>

    <!--
        The Kotlin client: a coroutine-native client for the frozen v1 protocol, and the one
        non-Java client with NO foreign-toolchain wrapper - Kotlin is a JVM language, builds with
        kotlin-maven-plugin from Central like any other plugin, and needs nothing installed that
        the JVM reactor does not already have. Kotlin wave of the language-proxy plan (astubbs#242).

        IT WRAPS THE JAVA gRPC TRANSPORT, and it did not always.

        Wave one reimplemented the session against parallel-consumer-proxy-protocol on three
        grounds, one measurable and two design. The measurable one was real and is now fixed at
        source: java-grpc test-depended on the ENGINE, so `-pl <this> -am` pulled
        parallel-consumer-proxy into the reactor and `bin/build.sh` would `clean` the very jar every
        other language's conformance test spawns as its sidecar. The engine dependency now lives in
        parallel-consumer-proxy-client-java-harness, a module nothing wraps. Verify rather than
        trust, before and after any dependency change here:

            ./mvnw -pl :parallel-consumer-proxy-client-kotlin -am validate

        which must not print parallel-consumer-proxy.

        The second ground - that wrapping would put Java's thread pool under a coroutine surface,
        one `runBlocking` per record - was also real, and was also fixed at source: the reference
        API now takes an AsyncRecordProcessor returning a CompletionStage, which a coroutine
        completes without any thread waiting. The third, that a wrapper could not answer the two
        known specification defects, is simply void: java-grpc answers both, and inheriting its
        answers is the point.

        What remains here is what is genuinely Kotlin. This module keeps its own spelling of the
        types where Kotlin says the thing better - nullability rather than Optional, default
        arguments rather than a builder, a sealed Outcome, one nullable PreviousFailure rather than
        two - and imports the reference API's own types where it does not. That boundary is one
        file, Bridge.kt, so the cost of being a Kotlin client stays visible and countable.

        It therefore depends on parallel-consumer-proxy-client-java-grpc and on nothing else of its
        own: protobuf, gRPC and the protocol module all arrive transitively through the transport,
        and no code in this module names any of them.
    -->

    <properties>
        <!--
            This module's real runtime floor is Java 17: the gRPC channel stack requires it, as
            the java-grpc module's pom explains at length. Kotlin's own jvmTarget below is set to
            match, so bytecode and claim agree.
        -->
        <release.target>17</release.target>

        <kotlin.version>2.4.10</kotlin.version>
        <kotlinx.coroutines.version>1.11.0</kotlinx.coroutines.version>

        <!--
            THE ONE TEST THAT NEEDS THE JVM-SIDE HARNESS, and the switch that lets it exist without
            a permanent reactor edge to the engine. Excluded by name in an ordinary build; the
            kotlin-e2e-harness profile below flips this to a pattern matching nothing, so
            -Dpc.foreignClients (which the module's CI row passes) runs it. Two things follow from
            that arrangement and neither is obvious: the exclusion is by CLASS NAME, so renaming
            the test silently re-enables it in a lane with no classpath file - and the test fails
            rather than skips when the file is missing, so that mistake is loud.
        -->
        <pc.kotlin.sidecar.test.exclude>**/OneRecordThroughTheSidecarTest.*</pc.kotlin.sidecar.test.exclude>
    </properties>

    <dependencies>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.jetbrains.kotlin</groupId>
            <artifactId>kotlin-stdlib</artifactId>
            <version>${kotlin.version}</version>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.jetbrains.kotlinx</groupId>
            <artifactId>kotlinx-coroutines-core</artifactId>
            <version>${kotlinx.coroutines.version}</version>
        </dependency>
        <!--
            The session, shared with every other JVM client: the stream, the handshake, the
            dispatch queue and its overflow rule, the verbatim token echo. The protocol module,
            protobuf and the gRPC channel stack all arrive through it, and nothing in this module
            names them - which is the check that this really is a wrapper and not a fork.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
            <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-client-java-grpc</artifactId>
            <version>${project.version}</version>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.jetbrains.kotlinx</groupId>
            <artifactId>kotlinx-coroutines-test</artifactId>
            <version>${kotlinx.coroutines.version}</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <profiles>
        <!--
            THE SIDECAR THE END-TO-END TEST DRIVES, AND WHY IT IS A PROFILE.

            The one end-to-end test spawns the real test-mode sidecar (TestModeMain, in the proxy
            module's TEST jar - it must never reach a client package) over the real wire, so it
            needs that jar, the proxy's main jar, and core's test jar underneath, plus a classpath
            file naming them. Declaring those OUTSIDE a profile would give this module a permanent
            reactor edge to the engine: `-pl <this> -am` would then build - and `bin/build.sh`
            would `clean` - the proxy module, whose target/ holds the sidecar jar that every other
            language's conformance test spawns. Scoped here, the edge exists only in the opt-in
            lane, which is the same arrangement the Python module uses and for the same reason.

            The three jars are reached through ONE dependency, the java-harness module, which holds
            them at compile scope for exactly this purpose. Restating them here would be a second
            copy of a set that has already drifted once; the next JVM client - Scala - declares the
            same single line.

            The remaining harness dependencies - awaitility, truth, logback - are inherited from
            the root pom by every module, so they are not restated. If the harness ever grows one
            that is not, the sidecar dies at startup with a NoClassDefFoundError, and the spawn
            code keeps the sidecar's stderr tail precisely so the failure names itself.
        -->
        <profile>
            <id>kotlin-e2e-harness</id>
            <activation>
                <property>
                    <name>pc.foreignClients</name>
                </property>
            </activation>
            <properties>
                <!-- matches no test class: the sidecar test runs in this lane -->
                <pc.kotlin.sidecar.test.exclude>**/NoTestIsExcludedInTheHarnessLane.*</pc.kotlin.sidecar.test.exclude>
            </properties>
            <dependencies>
                <dependency>
                    <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
                    <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-client-java-harness</artifactId>
                    <version>${project.version}</version>
                    <scope>test</scope>
                </dependency>
            </dependencies>
            <build>
                <plugins>
                    <plugin>
                        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                        <artifactId>maven-dependency-plugin</artifactId>
                        <!-- version from the root pom's pluginManagement -->
                        <executions>
                            <execution>
                                <id>sidecar-classpath</id>
                                <!-- after the dependencies above resolve, before the test phase
                                     that reads the file -->
                                <phase>generate-test-resources</phase>
                                <goals>
                                    <goal>build-classpath</goal>
                                </goals>
                                <configuration>
                                    <outputFile>${project.build.directory}/sidecar-classpath.txt</outputFile>
                                    <includeScope>test</includeScope>
                                </configuration>
                            </execution>
                        </executions>
                    </plugin>
                    <plugin>
                        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                        <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
                        <!-- version from the root pom -->
                        <configuration>
                            <!--
                                THE ENGINE IS THE SIDECAR'S CLASSPATH, NOT THIS TEST'S - and saying
                                so here is what keeps the CI row's own command working.

                                The dependencies above exist to be handed to a CHILD PROCESS; this
                                JVM needs none of them, and having them costs rather than helps. In
                                a reactor run (`-pl <this> -am`, which is exactly what the CI row
                                does) a `test-jar` dependency on a module that has not reached the
                                package phase resolves to a class directory that is not the one
                                holding its test classes - so core's JUnit
                                `TestExecutionListener` service file arrives without the listener
                                it names, and the fork dies before any test runs with
                                `ServiceConfigurationError: Provider ... MyRunListener not found`.
                                Verified both ways: red with `-am` before this stanza, green after.
                            -->
                            <classpathDependencyExcludes>
                                <classpathDependencyExclude>
                                    bz.stub.parallelconsumer:parallel-consumer-core
                                </classpathDependencyExclude>
                                <classpathDependencyExclude>
                                    bz.stub.parallelconsumer:parallel-consumer-proxy
                                </classpathDependencyExclude>
                            </classpathDependencyExcludes>
                        </configuration>
                    </plugin>
                </plugins>
            </build>
        </profile>
    </profiles>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-dependency-plugin</artifactId>
                <!-- version from the root pom's pluginManagement -->
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <!--
                            THE CONFORMANCE RUNNER'S CLASSPATH, in the DEFAULT build rather than a
                            profile.

                            scripts/conformance-runner is the executable the shared suite's registry
                            names, and a JVM client's "binary" is a JVM plus a resolved classpath -
                            so this file is what makes that wrapper a runnable thing. It is not in
                            the harness profile because the runner needs no engine: it spawns the
                            SIDECAR SHIM the suite hands it on its sidecar flag, which is the client
                            library's real spawn path, and the engine lives in the suite's own JVM.

                            Written at generate-test-resources, before test-compile emits the runner
                            beside it, so an ordinary `test-compile` of this module leaves both
                            halves current. Nothing regenerates it at run time, deliberately: the
                            reactor that runs the suite already builds this module, and a nested
                            `mvn` would rewrite class directories under the JVM executing the suite.
                        -->
                        <id>conformance-classpath</id>
                        <phase>generate-test-resources</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>build-classpath</goal>
                        </goals>
                        <configuration>
                            <outputFile>${project.build.directory}/conformance-classpath.txt</outputFile>
                            <!--
                                Test scope, so the runner's own JVM gets logback and the diagnostics
                                the suite attaches to a failure are readable. In the harness lane the
                                engine artifacts join it too and are simply unused - the runner never
                                names them, because it never spawns TestModeMain.
                            -->
                            <includeScope>test</includeScope>
                        </configuration>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.jetbrains.kotlin</groupId>
                <artifactId>kotlin-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>${kotlin.version}</version>
                <configuration>
                    <jvmTarget>${release.target}</jvmTarget>
                    <args>
                        <!--
                            EXPLICIT API MODE, strict. Every public declaration states its
                            visibility and its return type, so nothing reaches the published
                            surface by forgetting to write `internal` - the library equivalent of
                            the reference module's discipline about what may appear on the shared
                            API, enforced by the compiler instead of by review.
                        -->
                        <arg>-Xexplicit-api=strict</arg>
                    </args>
                </configuration>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <id>compile</id>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>compile</goal>
                        </goals>
                        <configuration>
                            <sourceDirs>
                                <sourceDir>${project.basedir}/src/main/kotlin</sourceDir>
                            </sourceDirs>
                        </configuration>
                    </execution>
                    <execution>
                        <id>test-compile</id>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>test-compile</goal>
                        </goals>
                        <configuration>
                            <sourceDirs>
                                <sourceDir>${project.basedir}/src/test/kotlin</sourceDir>
                            </sourceDirs>
                            <args combine.self="override">
                                <!-- tests are not a published surface; explicit API mode would
                                     only add ceremony to them -->
                            </args>
                        </configuration>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
                <!-- version from the root pom -->
                <configuration>
                    <excludes>
                        <!-- the root pom's own exclusions, restated: a child <excludes> replaces
                             the parent's list rather than adding to it -->
                        <exclude>**/integrationTest*/**/*.java</exclude>
                        <exclude>**/*IT.java</exclude>
                        <exclude>${pc.kotlin.sidecar.test.exclude}</exclude>
                    </excludes>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>

</project>
